page('index.html','Independent investigations','''<h1 class="visually-hidden">Civic Ledger: investigations in the public interest</h1><section class="lead-grid" data-section="lead-investigations" data-component="lead-grid"><article class="lead" data-section="lead-story" data-component="story-card"><a href="article.html"><img src="assets/ballot.svg" alt="Illustration of a marked ballot above a ballot box" width="600" height="350"></a><p class="eyebrow">Democracy / The Public Record</p><h2><a href="article.html">The List That Put Eligible Voters Under Review</a></h2><p>A matching tool promised a shortcut. In the fictional county of Linden, the people behind the names told a different story.</p><p class="byline">by Mara Vale and Eli North · Nov. 12, 2026</p></article><div class="lead-side">'''+card(1)+card(2)+'''</div></section><section class="band" data-section="biggest-stories"><h2>Our Biggest Stories</h2><div class="three-grid">'''+card(3)+card(4)+card(5)+'''</div></section><section class="band" data-section="browse-topics"><h2>Browse Topics</h2><div class="topic-heading"><h3><a href="democracy.html">Democracy →</a></h3><p>Following the decisions that shape public life.</p></div><div class="two-grid">'''+card(0)+card(3)+'''</div></section><section class="series-band" data-section="homepage-series"><p class="eyebrow">An ongoing series</p><h2>The Public Record</h2><p>Who can see government at work — and who gets left outside?</p><a href="democracy.html#series">Explore the series →</a></section>''')
page('democracy.html','Democracy','''<div class="page-intro" data-section="topic-introduction"><p class="eyebrow">Topics we cover</p><h1>Democracy</h1><p class="deck">Investigating how public decisions are made, who has influence and whether the rules work for everyone.</p></div><section class="band" data-section="featured-stories"><h2>Featured Stories</h2><div class="two-grid">'''+card(0)+card(3)+'''</div></section><section class="impact" data-section="reporting-impact"><p class="eyebrow">Fictional sample follow-up</p><h2>Impact of Our Reporting</h2><h3>A county opens its review process to the public</h3><p>In this invented follow-up, Linden’s records office publishes a plain-language guide after residents ask how to correct mismatched records. This is narrative demo content, not a claim of real impact.</p><a href="article.html#follow-up">Read the follow-up →</a></section><section id="series" class="band" data-section="topic-series"><h2>Series</h2><div class="two-grid"><article data-section="public-record-series" data-component="series-card"><h3>The Public Record</h3><p>Following the trail from a spreadsheet to a public decision.</p><a href="article.html">Read the featured investigation</a></article><article data-section="open-doors-series" data-component="series-card"><h3>Open Doors</h3><p>The practical barriers between a resident and a record.</p><a href="search.html?q=ledger">Find related reporting</a></article></div></section>''')
paragraphs=['At the kitchen table in her fictional Linden home, Lena Moss laid out a utility bill, a registration card and a handwritten note. All three showed the address where she had lived for years. A county list said her record needed another look.','The list came from a matching tool that compared names across public files. Similar names appeared beside one another, but the worksheet did not explain whether a match had been checked by a person. A suggestion had become a question that residents had to answer.','For this fictional investigation, our reporters reconstructed a small sample of records and interviewed invented residents and officials. The exercise illustrates a reporting method; it does not describe an actual election, vendor or county.','Moss was able to correct her record. Her neighbor, who worked nights, needed a second appointment. Both described the same problem: a notice told them what to bring, but not why their names had been selected.','A review can be useful when its limits are clear. A name match alone is not proof that two records describe the same person. Dates, addresses and the history of a record can change what an apparent match means.','The fictional county clerk said staff would add an explanation to future notices and preserve a record of each review. The proposed changes would let a resident ask a more specific question and understand the answer.']
page('article.html','The List That Put Eligible Voters Under Review','''<article data-section="article" data-component="article-detail"><header class="article-head" data-section="article-header"><p class="eyebrow"><a href="democracy.html">Democracy</a> / The Public Record</p><h1>The List That Put Eligible Voters Under Review</h1><p class="deck">A county bought a matching tool. Its records could not tell two neighbors apart. Residents were left to untangle the questions.</p><p>by Mara Vale and Eli North</p><p class="byline"><time datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · Fictional investigation</p></header><figure data-section="article-artwork" data-component="article-figure"><img src="assets/ballot.svg" alt="Original geometric illustration of a ballot and a ballot box" width="600" height="350"><figcaption>Original illustration for this fictional research sample.</figcaption></figure><div class="article-layout"><div class="prose" data-section="article-body">'''+''.join('<p>'+p+'</p>' for p in paragraphs[:3])+'''<h2>What a match leaves out</h2>'''+''.join('<p>'+p+'</p>' for p in paragraphs[3:])+'''<section id="follow-up" data-section="article-follow-up"><h2>What happened next</h2><p>In this fictional follow-up, the records office adds a public explanation of its review process. No actual outcome is claimed.</p></section><section data-section="contributors" data-component="contributor-list"><h2>Contributors</h2><p><strong>Mara Vale</strong> reports on public records in this fictional newsroom. <strong>Eli North</strong> contributes research and data review.</p></section><section data-section="republishing"><h2>Republish This Story</h2><p>This original demo text may be copied for evaluation. The panel provides a plain-text excerpt; it does not publish anywhere.</p><button type="button" data-open="republish-dialog" aria-haspopup="dialog">View republishing excerpt</button></section></div><aside data-section="most-read" data-component="most-read-list"><h2>Most Read</h2><ol><li><a href="search.html?q=clinic">The Waiting Room With No End</a></li><li><a href="search.html?q=climate">A Climate Promise Written in Fine Print</a></li><li><a href="article.html">The List That Put Eligible Voters Under Review</a></li></ol></aside></div></article><section class="band" data-section="related-reporting"><h2>More in Democracy</h2>'''+card(3)+'''</section><dialog id="republish-dialog" aria-labelledby="republish-title" data-section="republishing-dialog" data-component="republish-dialog"><button type="button" data-close>Close ×</button><h2 id="republish-title">Republishing excerpt</h2><label for="republish-text">Original demo text, available to select and copy</label><textarea id="republish-text" rows="8" readonly>The List That Put Eligible Voters Under Review — by Mara Vale and Eli North, Civic Ledger (fictional research demo). A county bought a matching tool. Its records could not tell two neighbors apart. Residents were left to untangle the questions.</textarea></dialog>''')
